  "notes": "Agent re-read after the Code Health refactor, 2026-11-07, under the owner directive transcribed in verification/reviews/rulings/agent-rereview-after-refactor-2026-11-07.md. This record is NOT an owner signature: it attests the agent's substantive reading only. It replaces a record naming the owner as reviewer, because that record's fingerprint no longer matched. Read client.response_binding_disposition and client.response_signer_authorization, both over mcp-re-client-core/src/response.rs. The production half of response.rs (everything above the test module) is byte-identical to the reviewed one; response_expectation.rs and delegated_evidence.rs have no diff. Only delegated_tests changed. The helpers it gained (sign_success, live_window, first_delegated_kid, bound_rejection, preflight_rejection, verify, verify_at, verify_pinned, verify_202, revoked_verdict) reproduce the inline setup of the old tests: custody from the same seeded factory (so a delegated kid is deterministic across instances), the same live 300-second signing window at NOW, an empty revocation set unless the test revokes one, and ResponseExpectation::for_signed with the issuer pin only where the test sets one. I diffed the changed tests that carry the claim and each keeps its assertions: the unpinned, root-pinned, other-root-pinned and delegated-kid-pinned cases for success, rejection and a 202; revocation by delegated kid, by issuer kid, by jti and of a rejection receipt, each expecting DelegationRevoked; the unsigned, direct-root and unbound-signature refusals; and the both-paths-failed refusal that returns the bound verdict. Request signing in the tests goes through build_signed_message with an McpMessage, as in the production proxy. The claim, scope and premises are unchanged. Evidence class as registered; V0 units are tested and probed, not machine-checked proofs. The mutation lane was not re-run by this reading; check-registries resolves every probe anchor.",
